WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.240
[Narrator] Pigeons.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.810
Some people love them.

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:06.790
Larry: Everybody thinks that
they're dirty and nasty,

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:08.020
but they're actually not.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:08.853
They're a dove.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:11.443
All they are is doves.

00:00:11.443 --> 00:00:14.000
Ah, you're a dove. Ah.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.500
[Narrator] Other people, not so much.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.360
Man: I hate 'em.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.710
They get way too close.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.850
I don't let people into the personal space

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:21.950
that pigeons get into.

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:23.120
They're just aggressive.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.120
[Narrator] But whatever
your stance may be,

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.795
if you live in a city, you
have to deal with them.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.550
While pigeons are clearly at home

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.680
in American cities like New York,

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.910
they're actually native to seaside cliffs

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.670
halfway around the world,

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.510
in North Africa, the
Middle East, and Europe,

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:47.100
where we domesticated
them 5,000 years ago.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.990
At first, we farmed them
for a source of protein,

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:51.080
like chicken.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.720
Then we bred them as messengers.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.140
In the 8th century BC, for example,

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:57.780
the Greeks used pigeons to send results

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.440
from the Olympic Games to nearby towns.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.400
And by the 16th century,

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.210
pigeons had reached the ultimate peak.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:08.210
Hobbyists began breeding
the birds for show.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.090
Akbar the Great, for example,

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.750
reportedly had 10,000 show pigeons

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.940
in his personal collection.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.868
Suffice it to say,

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:19.460
humans and pigeons were
inextricably linked.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.150
And that's why Europeans who
migrated to North America

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:25.750
in the 1600s brought some
of these birds with them.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.030
And surprise, surprise,

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:30.190
Elizabeth Carlen: They escaped,
and that's kind of what

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.850
formed these feral populations
in cities around the world.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.950
[Narrator] That's
biologist Elizabeth Carlen,

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.220
who studies pigeons at Fordham University.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.740
Once pigeons escaped,
their population exploded,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.710
especially in cities.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.160
Because, as Carlen says,

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.550
cities are essentially
tailor-made for these birds.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:51.180
For one, pigeons can thrive on human food,

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.280
unlike, say, robins or cardinals.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.480
Carlen: What we have
here is pigeons eating

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:58.310
it looks like rice and bagels

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:00.980
and probably doughnuts in there as well,

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.300
and that ability to
consume all this food waste

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.370
has really made them very
successful in cities.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:08.960
[Narrator] But it's not just our leftovers

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.270
they're noshing on.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.027
We also feed them.

00:02:12.027 --> 00:02:12.860
Larry: Spike.

00:02:14.147 --> 00:02:15.440
That's Spike.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:16.430
[Narrator] As a result,

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.050
pigeons spend a lot less
time searching for food

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.150
and a lot more time breeding,

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.420
which they can actually do without trees.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:26.940
In their native range, pigeons
nest on rocky seaside cliffs.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:28.903
Carlen: And cities often mimic that

00:02:28.903 --> 00:02:30.236
by having tall buildings

00:02:30.236 --> 00:02:33.070
and by having places for
pigeons to nest within that,

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:36.203
such as fire escapes or AC units

00:02:36.203 --> 00:02:38.520
or even just ledges that are built in

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.103
decoratively on the building

00:02:40.103 --> 00:02:42.028
all mimic those cliffs.

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.350
[Narrator] But there's another reason

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.060
why pigeons are so successful in cities.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.470
They're incredible navigators.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.500
Some of these birds
can find their way home

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.230
from nearly 1,000 kilometers away.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.550
And those navigation
skills serve them well

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:57.110
in a complex cityscape.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.010
Carlen: That is likely
linked to their ability

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.860
to find food within the city

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.480
and know where food
sources previously were

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.990
and go and check in those food sources.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.980
[Narrator] So how many
pigeons live in cities anyway?

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:12.850
In New York there's an adage:
One pigeon for every person.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:15.590
That would be more than 8 million birds.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:17.460
And whether or not that's true,

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.740
urbanites have decided on one thing:

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.240
The city isn't large enough
for the both of them.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:23.602
Man: I got nothin' for them.

00:03:23.602 --> 00:03:26.950
I don't got food. I don't
got money. I got nothin'.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:27.790
Leave me alone.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:29.750
[Narrator] In 2003, for example,

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.330
things got so bad in
New York's Bryant Park

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:35.460
that a professional falconer
was hired to scare them away.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.520
And it's not just American cities.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.160
In Bangkok for example,
officials have considered

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:43.440
imposing jail time for
people who feed the birds.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:44.640
But here's the thing:

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.740
As long as we have thriving cities,

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.220
pigeons will live in them.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:49.770
In fact, the only thing

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.600
that might control their populations,

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.200
aside from cleaning up,

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.725
is natural predators.

00:03:54.725 --> 00:03:56.270
Carlen: For a long time,
pigeons didn't have

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.140
natural predators within cities.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.830
[Narrator] That's thanks in
part to the insecticide DDT,

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.480
which Americans started
using in the 1940s.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.000
Carlen: The use of DDT
made eggshells very thin

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.350
and decreased the population
of raptors such as

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.530
peregrine falcons and Cooper's
hawks and red-tailed hawks.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.010
[Narrator] But in 1972,

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.860
the Environmental Protection
Agency banned DDT,

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:19.750
and as a result,

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:20.760
Carlen: Those predators are now

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.150
moving back into the city.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.040
[Narrator] And with so much to eat,

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.207
they probably won't be
leaving anytime soon.

